WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:12.701
[MUSIC]

00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:22.561
When we're talking about how leaders can
manage emotions in times of crisis, it's

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:27.470
very important to first understand what
we mean by manage and what we don't mean.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.710
Managing emotions means,
let's say, regulating emotions.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.800
It absolutely does not
mean suppressing emotions.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.350
The process of trying to suppress
an emotion is something we can do for

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.410
a short period of time,
but not indefinitely.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.644
It's very difficult, it drains us,
it takes a lot of energy,

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:48.097
it can actually be
counterproductive as well.

00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:52.204
The act of seeking to suppress an emotion
can exacerbate it, can heighten it,

00:00:52.204 --> 00:00:55.256
can remind us of the fact that
we're feeling something and

00:00:55.256 --> 00:00:57.630
it can come kind of roaring out at a time.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:02.580
So if we think of managing emotions
as seeking to regulate emotions,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.410
a couple of ways to address that.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.286
One is learning a little bit more
about our emotional tendencies and

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:11.490
how are we likely to respond
in certain circumstances.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.200
Sometimes that's very tactical.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.950
I know that I'm about to
go into a conversation or

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.721
a situation that I typically
experience as somewhat triggering.

00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:24.027
And let me just sort of prepare myself for
that, let me ensure that my calendar is

00:01:24.027 --> 00:01:28.320
a little bit clearer both before and
after this particular interaction.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.980
If I've got a particularly difficult
conversation or interaction,

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.420
make sure I'm getting a good night's
sleep in advance, things like that.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.080
There's also stuff that we can do
in the moment, that leaders can do,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.930
to regulate and manage emotions
more effectively in the moment.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:47.020
Some of that simply entails
ensuring that we're framing

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.550
the situation in a way that's going to be
most useful for our emotional response.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:55.746
Framing or cognitive appraisal in the sort
of term of art that psychologists

00:01:55.746 --> 00:02:00.616
use relates to the meanings that we
are assigning to a given situation,

00:02:00.616 --> 00:02:02.907
to a given set of circumstances.

00:02:02.907 --> 00:02:05.736
Am I assuming that this is
going to be a hostile interaction,

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:07.750
this is going to be contentious?

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.180
And if so, what am I basing that on?

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.022
Is that necessarily accurate or
am I just making some assumptions here?

00:02:14.022 --> 00:02:20.500
A more common term that we use in my field
as an executive coach is mental models.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.530
The mental models are basically the set of
assumptions that we make about the world

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.890
around us to help us make sense of the
world and navigate it more effectively.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.078
These models are largely accurate,
that's how we get through the day.

00:02:33.078 --> 00:02:35.450
And they're less accurate
in certain circumstances,

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.460
they're less accurate under stress.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.679
And can we step back and actually say,
well, what are my mental models?

00:02:40.679 --> 00:02:41.702
What are the assumptions and

00:02:41.702 --> 00:02:44.100
interpretations that I'm
bringing to the situation?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.707
And if they're not useful,
can I reframe the situation?

00:02:47.707 --> 00:02:51.752
And then we can take a number of steps to
build our capacity to regulate emotion

00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:52.384
over time.

00:02:52.384 --> 00:02:58.360
The cheesy acronym that I use in my
class at the GSB is messy, M-E-S-S.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.866
If we can commit to
a mindfulness practice, and

00:03:00.866 --> 00:03:04.451
meditation is the single most
effective kind of tool in that regard.

00:03:04.451 --> 00:03:07.865
There are a number of other
ways to pursue mindfulness,

00:03:07.865 --> 00:03:11.145
going on a reflective walk,
some forms of exercise.

00:03:11.145 --> 00:03:17.317
E for exercise, if we can get some
consistent, regular physical activity.

00:03:17.317 --> 00:03:20.297
S for sleep,
if we can get regular amounts of sleep and

00:03:20.297 --> 00:03:24.210
sufficient amounts of sleep and
higher quality amounts of sleep.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.760
And second S for chronic stress.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.737
If we can identify sources of chronic
stress in our life and diminish them,

00:03:31.737 --> 00:03:36.714
we're going to be much more effective
at emotion regulation in those moments

00:03:36.714 --> 00:03:38.139
when we need it most.

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:43.797
[MUSIC]

00:03:43.797 --> 00:03:48.907
So under stressful conditions, like those
that we're living on a day-to-day basis

00:03:48.907 --> 00:03:53.737
in the COVID-19 era, we often experience
what's known as a threat response,

00:03:53.737 --> 00:03:56.940
or a fight, flight or freeze response.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.120
And under those conditions,
one of the ways that it manifests itself

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.810
is this sense of urgency,
the need to hurry up.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.864
And although in certain circumstances
that's actually quite useful,

00:04:08.864 --> 00:04:12.527
because obviously we evolve this
response in a way to help us survive.

00:04:12.527 --> 00:04:17.737
The dilemma is that very rarely these
days do we experience a literal threat

00:04:17.737 --> 00:04:22.715
to our physical safety that requires
an immediate physical response.

00:04:22.715 --> 00:04:27.587
We often experience a situation that
we interpret as a threat to our

00:04:27.587 --> 00:04:31.590
physical safety and
we speed up in response to it.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:36.380
And that speed, that sense of urgency
is actually counterproductive.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.620
So the impulse to hurry should, I think,

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.630
often be interpreted as
a signal to slow down.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:46.310
If we can train ourselves to
interrogate that sense of urgency and

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.070
ask ourselves, is that really useful here?

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.640
Is it really going to be helpful here?

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.200
Or is it going to spur me to potentially
some counterproductive action?

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.362
And remind ourselves to slow down and
assess what's happening a little

00:04:57.362 --> 00:05:01.169
bit more deliberately, we're likely to
navigate the situation more effectively.

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:06.707
[MUSIC]

00:05:06.707 --> 00:05:10.414
I often work with professionals,
with the leaders of my practice,

00:05:10.414 --> 00:05:13.285
are going through some kind
of transitional phase.

00:05:13.285 --> 00:05:17.690
And we often have a negative
emotional response to endings.

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:23.400
This is kind of reflected in a line
from an old Tom Cruise movie Cocktail.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.762
Tom Cruise plays a bartender, and
in that movie, he says at one point,

00:05:27.762 --> 00:05:31.194
everything ends badly,
otherwise it would never end.

00:05:31.194 --> 00:05:35.267
And there's some truth to that,
because endings,

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:39.380
in some sense,
they're all symbolic deaths.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.020
They remind us that this
existence will end.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.656
And whatever we believe about
what transcends this existence,

00:05:44.656 --> 00:05:46.380
we're very attached to this existence.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.824
And it could be quite dislocating,

00:05:48.824 --> 00:05:52.417
it could be unnerving to
be reminded of that ending.

00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:56.901
And yet, I would suggest that our negative
emotional response to endings leads us to

00:05:56.901 --> 00:06:00.670
kind of lean away from endings
as opposed to leaning into them.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:04.708
And we thus miss opportunities to
practice, to really gain something from

00:06:04.708 --> 00:06:08.554
each ending and learn something from it,
that would help us prepare for

00:06:08.554 --> 00:06:13.066
the much larger series of endings that we
will encounter over the rest of our lives.

00:06:13.066 --> 00:06:18.590
I'm reminded of a line from Nietzsche,
that also seems very relevant here.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:21.870
The end of a melody is not its goal,
nonetheless,

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.730
had the melody not reached its end it
would not have attained its goal either.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.610
And I think that's true for
every experience we find ourselves in.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.320
It's true for the course that I teach.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.136
The end of the class is not the point,
but if we hadn't reached the end,

00:06:34.136 --> 00:06:36.436
we wouldn't have gained the experience.

00:06:36.436 --> 00:06:38.745
It's true for
the students that I work with.

00:06:38.745 --> 00:06:42.645
The end of graduate school is not the
point, but if they didn't reach the end,

00:06:42.645 --> 00:06:44.305
they wouldn't have achieved the goal.

00:06:44.305 --> 00:06:48.136
And I think we can apply that in
a much larger philosophical or

00:06:48.136 --> 00:06:50.399
even spiritual sense to our lives.

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:54.212
Obviously, the point of a life
is not to reach the ending.

00:06:54.212 --> 00:06:58.072
And yet, if we hadn't reached the ending,
if this existence weren't finite in some

00:06:58.072 --> 00:07:01.492
sense, I don't believe that it would
have the same sense of meaning.

00:07:01.492 --> 00:07:04.280
So I believe that it's important for
us to acknowledge

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:09.020
the finite nature of all experience,
and in some sense, recognize and

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:13.830
lean into the very uncomfortable,
unnerving emotions that that stirs up.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.127
In part because it's good practice,

00:07:16.127 --> 00:07:19.366
it helps us extract something
that we can learn from.

00:07:19.366 --> 00:07:23.665
It helps us learn something from every
experience that ends in a way of

00:07:23.665 --> 00:07:27.011
preparing us for
the larger endings that are to come.

00:07:27.011 --> 00:07:35.782
[MUSIC]

